  preview ={C:\Users\91917\Documents\GitHub\vedantphad.github.io\assets\img\publication_preview\Phad_BookChapter_frontpage.pdf},
  abstract = {The development of effective viral vaccines has been a cornerstone of modern medicine. Over recent years, innovative approaches have revolutionized the field of vaccine development by diminishing the effects of infections and saving countless lives in the process. Although traditional vaccine technologies have been used to combat a variety of bacterial and viral pathogens, there exist instances where these conventional methods have encountered challenges in cases involving complex viral antigens, persistent infections, and fast-mutating pathogens characterized by substantial genetic variation. This chapter explores the latest frontiers in viral vaccine development, focusing on DNA vaccines, mRNA vaccines, protein subunit vaccines, the novel technologies of vaccine development by reverse genetics approaches, and reverse vaccinology. These emerging technologies hold the promise of faster, safer, and more adaptable solutions to combat both existing and emerging viral threats. Through a comprehensive examination of these novel approaches, we aim to provide insights into the future of viral vaccine development.}
